Software Requirement Specification (SRS)
PrepFlow

Version: v1.0

Status: Draft

Author: Tejas Nere

1. Purpose

The purpose of PrepFlow is to provide students with a structured learning platform where all study resources are organized chapter-wise, allowing students to focus on studying instead of searching for content.

This document defines the functional and non-functional requirements of the system.

2. Scope

PrepFlow is a web-based learning platform designed primarily for Class 11, Class 12, and MHT CET students.

The platform will provide:

Structured learning roadmap
Organized study resources
Progress tracking
Analytics
Admin Content Management System (CMS)

Future versions may support additional competitive examinations.

3. User Roles
3.1 Student

A student can:

Browse subjects
Open chapters
Access study resources
Mark resources as completed
Track overall progress
View analytics
Search chapters

A student cannot:

Add resources
Edit resources
Delete resources
Modify platform content
3.2 Administrator

An administrator can:

Login securely
Manage subjects
Manage chapters
Manage resources
Upload PDFs
Hide or publish content
Edit metadata
Manage tags
Reorder resources
Preview student view

The administrator should never need to modify source code to update platform content.

4. Functional Requirements
FR-1 User Authentication

The system shall provide administrator authentication.

Student authentication will be introduced in future versions.

FR-2 Dashboard

The dashboard shall display:

Overall Progress
Subject Progress
Continue Learning
Recently Opened Chapters
Search
Analytics
FR-3 Subject Management

The system shall support:

Create Subject
Edit Subject
Delete Subject
Hide Subject
Publish Subject
FR-4 Chapter Management

The administrator shall be able to:

Create Chapter
Edit Chapter
Delete Chapter
Reorder Chapters
Hide Chapter
Publish Chapter
FR-5 Resource Management

Each chapter can contain multiple learning resources.

Examples:

Playlist
Notes
Formula Sheet
Previous Year Questions

Future resource types should be supported without changing application code.

FR-6 Resource Completion

Students shall be able to mark individual resources as completed.

Completion should automatically update:

Chapter Progress
Subject Progress
Overall Progress
FR-7 Progress Tracking

The platform shall calculate:

Resource Progress

↓

Chapter Progress

↓

Subject Progress

↓

Overall Progress

All progress values should update automatically.

FR-8 Search

Students shall be able to search by:

Subject
Chapter
Resource Name

Future search filters may include:

Tags
Teacher
Difficulty
FR-9 Analytics

The platform shall provide:

Overall Completion Percentage
Circular Progress Indicator
Subject-wise Progress
Chapter Completion Status
FR-10 File Upload

The administrator shall upload files through the CMS.

Files shall be stored in Supabase Storage.

Only file URLs shall be stored inside MongoDB.

FR-11 Metadata

Every chapter and resource may contain optional metadata.

Examples:

Estimated Study Time
Difficulty
Teacher
Duration
Importance

Metadata should remain completely optional.

FR-12 Tags

Administrators shall create custom tags.

Examples:

High Weightage
Beginner
Important
Revision
Must Do

Tags should not be hardcoded.

FR-13 Visibility

Every subject, chapter and resource shall support:

Draft
Published
Hidden

Students should only view published content.

FR-14 Ordering

Administrators shall change display order without changing code.

Resources should support:

Drag and Drop
Move Up
Move Down
5. Non Functional Requirements

The platform should be:

Fast

Responsive

Scalable

Secure

Simple

Maintainable

Mobile Friendly

Easy to Use

Future Ready

6. Performance Requirements

The system should:

Load dashboards quickly.

Load chapters efficiently.

Support thousands of resources.

Support future growth without architectural changes.

7. Security Requirements

Administrator pages should require authentication.

Students should never access CMS pages.

Uploaded files should be securely stored.

Database should never expose sensitive information.

8. Storage Requirements

MongoDB Atlas shall store:

Subjects
Chapters
Resources
Users
Progress
Metadata

Supabase Storage shall store:

Notes
PDFs
Formula Sheets
Images
9. Architecture Requirements

The application shall follow a client-server architecture.

Frontend

↓

Backend API

↓

Database

↓

Storage

Content must always be served through APIs.

Frontend shall never contain hardcoded learning resources.

10. Dynamic Content System

All platform content must be dynamically loaded from the database.

Administrators should update platform content without modifying source code.

The frontend should automatically render new content received from the backend.

11. Content Management Philosophy

Every editable item should be manageable from the Admin CMS.

Examples include:

Subjects
Chapters
Resources
Metadata
Tags
Visibility
Display Order

The administrator should never require Visual Studio Code for content updates.

12. Future Scalability

The architecture should support:

Additional Subjects
New Exams
New Resource Types
AI Features
Battle Mode
Leaderboards
Community Features

without requiring major structural changes.

13. Design Philosophy

Student Interface

Minimal

Simple

Organized

Distraction Free

Admin Interface

Powerful

Flexible

Content Focused

Highly Configurable

14. System Principles

Everything should be data driven.

Nothing should be hardcoded.

Frontend should render data received from the backend.

The backend should remain independent of frontend implementation.

Content updates should happen through the Admin CMS.

15. Acceptance Criteria

The first version of PrepFlow shall be considered complete when:

Students can browse all subjects.
Students can open chapters.
Students can access learning resources.
Students can track progress.
Students can view analytics.
Administrators can manage all content.
Administrators can upload files.
Administrators can edit platform data without changing source code.
The platform is responsive on desktop and mobile.
16. Final Principle

PrepFlow should allow students to focus on learning and administrators to focus on content, not code.